BECPOINT|id=GX-1/1000000|q=1/1000000|N_max=16|grid_N=1, 2, 3, 8, 16|role=q -> 0 boundary control|json=0
BECPOINT|id=TRUNC|meaning=truncated model, levels e <= L enumerated state by state|q=2/5, 1/2, 9/10, 99/100|N=1, 2, 3, 4|L=1, 2, 3 (4, 10 and 20 modes, at most 8855 states)|runs=48|json=0
BECPOINT|id=TS|meaning=condensed scaling points, N = n^3, interval mode|points=t = 1/2 and 7/10 at n = 8, 16, 32, 64 (N = 512, 4096, 32768, 262144); t = 9/10 at n = 16, 32|count=10|rule=every TS point has t <= t_M8(N) and a Chernoff certificate (BEC-T03, BEC-T11)|json=0
BECPOINT|id=TC|meaning=critical control, inside the critical window by design|point=(t, n) = (9/10, 8), N = 512, M = (N - S)/sqrt(V) about 0.48|json=0
BECPOINT|id=DY|meaning=interval mode at a zero-width dyadic input, where the exact answer is known|points=q = 1/2 and 3/4 at N = 2, 16, 64, 256, plus q = 3/4 at N = 512|count=9|json=0
BECPOINT|id=CS|meaning=CI short points, run on every push with the planted faults|points=exact: q = 1/2 and 3/4 at N <= 32; DY at N = 2, 16, 64; interval: TS (1/2, 8), TS (7/10, 8) and TC|json=0
BECPOINT|id=CAL|meaning=calibration points for rule BUDGET; excluded points, never row points|points=exact mode q = 5/8, N = 128; interval mode t = 5/7 at n = 15 (N = 3375) and n = 32 (N = 32768); t n = 75/7 and 160/7, not row values|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D1|text=closed forms by the cycle index of S_N: Z_N = sum over cycle types prod_k z_k^{c_k}/(k^{c_k} c_k!), z_k = (1-q^k)^-3. Z_2 = (z_1^2 + z_2)/2 = (1+3q^2)/((1-q)^6 (1+q)^3), since (1+q)^3 + (1-q)^3 = 2 + 6q^2. Z_3 = (z_1^3 + 3 z_1 z_2 + 2 z_3)/6 = P3/((1-q)^9 (1+q)^3 (1+q+q^2)^3), P3 = (b^3 c^3 + 3 a^3 c^3 + 2 a^6 b^3)/6 = 1 + 3q^2 + 7q^3 + 6q^4 + 6q^5 + 10q^6 + 3q^7 with a = 1-q, b = 1+q, c = 1+q+q^2; P3(1) = 36, P3(-1) = 4 and the numerator is 2 a^6 b^3 != 0 where c = 0, so the form is reduced. E_1 = q(3 - 3q + q^2)/a^3, E_2 = (6q^2 - 3q^4 + q^6)/(a^6 b^3), E_3 = (10q^3 + 6q^4 + 15q^5 + 7q^6 + 3q^7 - 9q^8 + q^9 + 3q^11)/(a^9 b^3 c^3). The forms agree with both constructions as rational functions (lane EXACT; judge-math by power series beyond the degree bound)|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D2|text=two constructions, one generating function: sum_N Z_N x^N = prod over all states (1 - x q^e)^-1 = (1-x)^-1 E(x), E(x) = prod_{e>=1} (1 - x q^e)^-g_e = sum_m E_m x^m; x d/dx of the logarithm gives sum_k x^k Z_1(q^k) and sum_k x^k (Z_1(q^k) - 1), the owner's two recursions; hence Z_N = sum_{m<=N} E_m and E_m = Z_m - Z_{m-1}|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D3|text=survival, mean, variance: removing k ground-state bosons maps the N-boson states with N_0 >= k one to one, at equal energy, onto the (N-k)-boson states, so P(N_0 >= k) = Z_{N-k}/Z_N, P(N_0 = N) = 1/Z_N and P(N_0 = j) = E_{N-j}/Z_N; E[N_0] = sum_{k>=1} P(N_0 >= k) and E[N_0^2] = sum_{k>=1} (2k-1) P(N_0 >= k), which give the owner's mean and variance; for a level-e state P(n_i >= k) = q^(k e) Z_{N-k}/Z_N. Cancellation-free form for interval arithmetic: with F_j = P(N_0 >= j) and G_j = 1 - F_j = sum_{i<j} E_{N-i}/Z_N, Var(N_0) = sum_l F_l (G_l + 2 sum_{j<l} G_j), every term positive, O(N), equal to the owner's form exactly|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D4|text=limit law (the Maxwell-demon picture of lane SCALING): pi(m) = E_m/E(1) is the law of the total occupation of independent geometric excited states at chemical potential 0, with mean S(q) and variance V(q); the canonical N_ex law is pi conditioned on m <= N. So Delta1 = S - <N_ex>_N = sum_{m>N} (m-S) pi(m)/P(m <= N) > 0; widening the window to N+1 adds mass above the current mean, so <N_ex>_N strictly increases with N and stays below S. Exactly V - Var_N = (D2 - V T0)/(1 - T0) + D1^2/(1 - T0)^2 with T0 = sum_{m>N} pi(m), D1 = sum_{m>N} (m-S) pi(m), D2 = sum_{m>N} (m-S)^2 pi(m), so Var_N < V when N + 1 - S >= sqrt(V); Popoviciu gives Var_N <= N^2/4. Hence 1 - f_N = S/N - Delta1/N: the fraction deficit is O(1/N) with exact coefficient S(q), which is the owner's O(1/N); what is left after it, Delta1, falls geometrically (D8)|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D5|text=log-concavity and the grand-canonical bound: a positive sequence a without internal zeros is log-concave iff its Toeplitz matrix (a_{i-j}) is totally positive of order 2; a product of such matrices is TP2 by the Cauchy-Binet formula, so convolutions of log-concave sequences are log-concave (S. G. Hoggar 1974 states the polynomial case; cited as context, BECCITE R5); geometric laws are log-concave, partial sums are convolution with (1, 1, 1, ...), and pointwise limits keep a_m^2 >= a_{m-1} a_{m+1}, so E_m and Z_m are log-concave and G(k) = P(N_0 >= k) is log-concave in k. Against G*(k) = r^k with the same mean mu, log G - log G* is concave and zero at k = 0 and the sums agree, so G - G* changes sign once, from + to -; with the increasing weights 2k-1 this gives E[N_0^2] < E*[N_0^2], i.e. Var_C < mu(mu+1), strictly, since the canonical law has finite support (checked independently by judge-math)|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D6|text=q -> 0: states up to excitation energy n need at most n excited bosons, so <N_ex>_N and S(q) agree through q^N (S = 3q + 9q^2 + 13q^3 + 24q^4 + ...); the first difference comes from N+1 bosons in the three first excited states, C(N+3, 2) configurations, and D4 gives S - <N_ex>_N = (N+1) C(N+3,2) q^(N+1) (1 + O(q)) and V - Var_N = (N+1)^2 C(N+3,2) q^(N+1) (1 + O(q)); Var_N = 3q + 12q^2 + ... for N >= 2, 3q - 12q^2 + ... at N = 1; P(N_0 = N) = 1/Z_N > 1 - S(q) by Markov's inequality on N_ex|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D7|text=q -> 1: z_1 = 1/eps exactly and every z_k = O(1/eps), so a cycle type with N - r cycles is O(eps^r) relative to the leading one; with w = eps/(1+q)^3, Z_N = (z_1^N/N!)(1 + C(N,2) w + O(eps^2)); then <N_0> = N eps + N(N-1) eps^2 (1 - (1+q)^-3) + O(eps^3), Var_C = <N_0> + N(N-2) eps^2 + O(eps^3), Var_GC - Var_C = 2N eps^2 + O(eps^3), R = 1 - 2 eps + O(N eps^2); the expansion parameter is eps = (1-q)^3, not 1-q|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D8|text=pole form: E(x) = (1 - xq)^-3 F(x), F analytic for abs(x) < q^-2 (next singularity of order 6 at x = q^-2), so E_m = (q^m/2) H(1) [(m+1)(m+2) - (2m+3) mu1 + mu2] + O(m^5 q^(2m)) with H(1) = prod_{n>=1} (1-q^n)^-g_{n+1}; dividing by E(1), pi(m) = (G/2) q^m [...], G = prod_{n>=1} (1-q^n)^-(n+2) since g_{n+1} - g_n = n+2; inserted in D4 this gives Delta1 and Delta2 as finite sums; leading behaviour Delta1 ~ (G q/(2(1-q))) N^3 q^N, geometric; the relative error is about est = abs(C2/H(1)) N^3 q^N/60, C2 the weight of the x = q^-2 singularity (C2/H(1) = -(q^3/(1-q)^3) prod_{n>=3} (1-q^(n-2))^-g_n/H(1)); actual over estimate was 1.5 at excluded q = 1/5 and 0.03 to 0.06 at excluded q = 3/5|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D9|text=Mellin: S(x) = sum_{r>=1} C(r+2,2)/(e^{rx} - 1) has transform (1/2) Gamma(s) zeta(s) [zeta(s-2) + 3 zeta(s-1) + 2 zeta(s)] (lane SCALING's D4 dropped the factor zeta(s); its series was right). The residues at s = 3, 2, 1 (double), 0, -1 and -3 (s = -2 gives 0) give S = zeta(3) x^-3 + (3/2) zeta(2) x^-2 + x^-1 (ln(1/x) + gamma - 19/24) + 5/16 - (19/2880) x - x^3/113400 + R_S, with -19/24 = (1/2) zeta(-1) + (3/2) zeta(0); the next residue (s = -5) gives the first omitted term -19 x^5/304819200. V(x) has transform (1/2) Gamma(s) zeta(s-1) [same bracket]; the residues at s = 3, 2 (double), 1, 0 and -2 give V = zeta(2) x^-3 + x^-2 [(3/2)(ln(1/x) + gamma + 1) - 1/4 + zeta(2)] - x^-1/2 + 5/96 + x^2/19200 + R_V; the next residue (s = -4) gives x^4/1016064. Both series diverge for every x > 0: the residues at s = 1 - 2n grow like (2n)!/(2 pi)^(4n). They are asymptotic only; at the excluded and row q the residual equals the first omitted term to 2 or 3 digits. With x = c3/(t N^(1/3)): zeta(3)/x^3 = t^3 N and (3/2) zeta(2)/x^2 = C t^2 N^(2/3), so 1 - f_N = t^3 + C t^2 N^(-1/3) + (t/c3)(ln(t N^(1/3)/c3) + gamma - 19/24) N^(-2/3) + (5/16)/N - ... - Delta1/N, and Y_N = C t^2 + D_N - N^(-2/3) Delta1 with D_N = (t/c3)(ln(t N^(1/3)/c3) + gamma - 19/24) N^(-1/3) + (5/16) N^(-2/3) - (19/2880)(c3/t) N^(-1) - (c3/t)^3 N^(-5/3)/113400; likewise Var/N = (zeta(2)/zeta(3)) t^3 + (t^2/c3^2)[(3/2) ln(t N^(1/3)/c3) + (3/2)(1 + gamma) + zeta(2) - 1/4] N^(-1/3) - (t/(2 c3)) N^(-2/3) + (5/96)/N + (c3/t)^2 N^(-5/3)/19200 - Delta2/N (all 13 coefficients re-derived by judge-math)|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D10|text=one-body matrix and the field: exp(-beta H) P_N/Z_N is diagonal in the Fock basis of oscillator states, so <a_i^+ a_j> = delta_ij <n_i>; with D3, lambda_e = sum_{k>=1} q^(k e) P(N_0 >= k) = (q^e/(1-q^e))(1 - E[q^(e N_0)]) for each level-e state; lambda_0 = <N_0> is the largest eigenvalue (Penrose-Onsager) and lambda_e decreases strictly in e; the excited trace sum_k (Z_1(q^k) - 1) P(N_0 >= k) = N - <N_0> follows from construction 2 summed over m <= N; the gap q/(1-q) - lambda_1 = (q/(1-q)) E[q^(N_0)] lies in (0, Delta1/3): since <N_ex> = sum_{e>=1} g_e lambda_e and S = sum_{e>=1} g_e q^e/(1-q^e), Delta1 = sum_{e>=1} g_e (q^e/(1-q^e)) E[q^(e N_0)], a sum of positive terms whose e = 1 term is 3 times the gap; the ratio gap/Delta1 tends to 1/3 from below, since at leading pole order the e >= 2 terms carry one power of N fewer (the exact ratio was 0.118 to 0.317 at the sampled N, at the excluded q = 1/5, 1/3, 2/9, 3/5, 2/3: judge-math at 2/9, the final critic, fix-pass f5_gap.py; verifier round 1 found 0.097 at (2/3, N = 30) to 0.320 at (1/5, N = 40), and second-pass s1_compute.py agrees; always below 1/3 and rising with N); a pole-law estimate of the gap must sum the exact convolution E_m = sum_i C(m-i+2, 2) q^(m-i) F_i (E(x) = (1 - xq)^-3 F(x), D8) over m <= N, which gives sum_{m<=N} q^-m E_m = sum_{i<=N} C(N-i+3, 3) q^-i F_i, about (H(1)/6)(w^3 + (3 k2 - 1) w - k3) with w = N + 2 - mu1, k2 = mu2 - mu1^2 and k3 = sum_{n>=1} g_{n+1} r(1+r)/(1-r)^3, r = q^n, the cumulants of the law F_i q^-i/H(1); so gap is about (q/(1-q)) q^N (G/6)(w^3 + (3 k2 - 1) w - k3); this matched the exact gap to 4 digits or better wherever M = (N - S)/sqrt(V) >= 5 at those five excluded q; summing the pole form of pi(m) over every m <= N, as the first synthesis did, is wrong, because the form fails at small m; <a_0> = Tr(rho_N a_0) = 0 because a_0 maps the N-boson sector into the (N-1)-boson sector|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D11|text=monotonicity in q for N <= 6 (lane EXACT): the derivative numerators of every Z_{N-k}/Z_N (symbolic recursion, reduced rational functions, degrees 2 to 100) have no root in (0,1) by exact Sturm sequences after removing the factors q and 1-q and taking the squarefree part; the sign at the excluded q = 1/5 is negative, so every survival P(N_0 >= k) and <N_0> strictly decrease with q for N <= 6; 1/Z_N decreases for every N because Z_N has positive coefficients|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D12|text=Chernoff truncation bound (lane SCALING D6): for 1 < z < 1/q and m >= N+1, z^(m-N-1) >= 1, so with R(z) = exp(sum_k (z^k - 1) c_k/k), c_k = (1-q^k)^-3 - 1, L = sum_k z^k c_k, L2 = sum_k k z^k c_k and A = z^-(N+1) R(z): P(N_ex > N) <= A, Delta1 <= B1 = A L/(1-A) and Delta2 <= B2 = A (L^2 + L2)/(1-A) + B1^2 (the last when N + 1 - S >= sqrt(V)); checked against exact Delta1 at excluded q: it holds, loose by a factor 7 to 50|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D13|text=interval mode: dyadic numbers m 2^e with a p-bit mantissa; lower pass from q_lo with every result rounded down and every divisor rounded up, upper pass mirrored from q_hi; all terms positive, so Z_m and E_m are enclosed without cancellation; f_N = [S_lo/(N Z_hi), S_hi/(N Z_lo)] with numerator and denominator from opposite passes, so no monotonicity of f in q is needed; lambda_1 the same way; Var by the cancellation-free form of D3. Error propagation with u = 2^(1-p) and w <= 2u the relative width of the q input after outward rounding (guaranteed because q_N is certified to 2^-130 before the rounding to p bits, BEC-K03): Z_1(q^k) has relative error <= 3(w+u)/x + 6u because k q^k/(1-q^k) <= 1/x; per step eps_m <= eps_(m-1) + 3(w+u)/x + (m+8)u; so W_N <= W_bound = f u (36N/x + 2N^2 + 36N + 4) to first order, rigorous with u replaced by u/(1 - (N^2+8N)u); the 2N^2 term (sequential sums) dominates at large N; this bound is derived for construction 1, which gives engine A's f bracket; construction 2, which engine B's interval mode runs, weights by w(y) = y(3 - 3y + y^2)/(1-y)^3, whose relative error for small y = q^k is about k(w+u), with k up to m, not suppressed by y as in Z_1(q^k), so the per-step term above does not cover it and no bound is derived here for engine B's brackets (BEC-T01 reads their W_N/W_bound and reports it); the certification never relies on the bound, which only predicts the width; inward rounding inverts the bracket as soon as one operation is inexact|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D14|text=constants and enclosures: zeta(3) by Apery's alternating series (5/2) sum (-1)^(k+1)/(k^3 C(2k,k)) (consecutive partial sums bracket it; engine A and the reader) and by the Amdeberhan-Zeilberger series (1/64) sum (-1)^k (k!)^10 (205k^2+250k+77)/((2k+1)!)^5 (engine B); zeta(2) = 3 sum 1/(k^2 C(2k,k)), term ratio below 1/4, tail <= (4/3) next term, with no pi, in engine A and the reader; engine B forms zeta(2) = pi^2/6 from its own pi, pi/4 = atan(1/2) + atan(1/3), each arctangent bracketed by consecutive partial sums of its alternating series, so engine B's zeta(2) is independent of engine A's series; engine A computes no pi; cube roots by integer bracketing r^3 <= X < (r+1)^3 at scale 2^240; C by outward division; gamma by the Euler-Maclaurin series of H_n - ln n, which envelops it for f(x) = 1/x since every even derivative keeps one sign: consecutive truncations H_n - ln n - 1/(2n) + sum_{k=1..K} B_{2k}/(2k n^{2k}) bracket gamma (n = 100, K = 10, width 3e-42), and the n = 50 bracket must intersect it; ln(r) = k ln 2 + 2 atanh((r'-1)/(r'+1)) after reduction r = 2^k r', r' in [1, 2), ln 2 = 2 atanh(1/3), tail y^(2J+1)/((2J+1)(1-y^2)); without the reduction q = 1/10^6 gives only [7.95, 631.2] after 400 terms; q_N: N = n^3 removes the cube root of N, x = c3/(t n), exp(-x) for 0 < x < 1 from consecutive alternating partial sums (engine A) or the positive series with remainder (engine B), rounded outward to 2^-128; the log check -ln q_lo >= x_hi and -ln q_hi <= x_lo; elementary cross-checks: zeta(3) in sum_{k<=1000} k^-3 + [1/(2 1001^2), 1/(2 1000^2)] and zeta(2) in sum_{k<=1000} k^-2 + [1/1001, 1/1000] by the integral test|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D15|text=critical region (lane SCALING D10): the condensed law needs N - S(q_N) >> sqrt(V(q_N)) and x (N - S) >> 1; t_c(N) solves S(q_N(t)) = N on the Mellin form, about 1 - C/(3 N^(1/3)); the Gaussian window is about 0.39/sqrt(N) wide, but the exponential right tail of the limit law from the three lowest excited states makes the Gaussian margin optimistic; the truncated-Gaussian value of D4's Delta1 is sigma phi(M)/Phi(M), sigma = sqrt(V), and the exact Delta1 was about 2.2 times it (2.17) at excluded (t, n) = (4/5, 6), M = 2.97, and 0.90 to 2.55 times it at excluded q = 2/3, N = 32 to 56, M = -0.41 to 2.84, the factor growing with M (fix-pass f1_gauss.py, exact Decimal and Fraction recursions at those excluded points only); the factors 25 and 0.5 to 6 quoted earlier came from sigma (phi(M) - M (1 - Phi(M)))/Phi(M), which is the mean of (m - N)+ and not the D4 quantity; the variance of the truncated Gaussian, sigma^2 (1 - M phi/Phi - (phi/Phi)^2), was right; so rows use t <= t_M8(N), where M = (N - S)/sqrt(V) = 8, and a Chernoff certificate (D12); rows also avoid x > 0.36 (Mellin accuracy not calibrated) and, at fixed q, pole reads below about S + 4.5 sqrt(V)|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D16|text=denominator theorem and exact integer schemes (lane ENGINE): Z_N(q) = P_N(q)/prod_{k<=N} (1-q^k)^3 with P_N an integer polynomial of degree <= 3N(N-1)/2 (by induction: prod_{j=N-k+1..N} (1-q^j)^3/(1-q^k)^3 is an integer polynomial, since a run of k integers holds a multiple of k and (1-q^k) divides (1-q^j) when k divides j); that induction shows only that N P_N is an integer polynomial; P_N itself has integer coefficients because Z_N is a power series in q with integer coefficients (it counts states by energy), so P_N = Z_N prod_{k<=N} (1-q^k)^3 is a product of integer power series; at q = a/b, Z_N = b^(3N) P~_N(a, b)/D_N, so den(Z_N) divides D_N and b^(3N) divides X_N = Z_N D_N. Scheme A: X_n = Z_n D_M (M >= n), n X_n = sum_{k=1..n} b^(3k) X_(n-k)/c_k^3, every term an integer. Scheme B: Y_m = E_m D_m, R_(m,k) = D_m/D_(m-k), m Y_m = sum_k (b^(3k) - c_k^3)(R_(m,k)/c_k^3) Y_(m-k); X_N = sum_m Y_m D_N/D_m by Horner. Engine A runs scheme A: M(M+3)/2 divisions per run, every remainder checked, so a wrong theorem prints a GUARD row and never agrees silently. Scheme B is kept as the derivation of the integers Y_m = E_m D_m, which engine B recovers by CRT (D19), not by division|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D17|text=bit lengths and cost (lane ENGINE D5, D6): bits(D_N) = (3/2) N(N+1) log2 b + 3 sum_k log2(1 - q^k), computed exactly; the reduced denominator loses only gcd(P~, D_N), 0 to 2.5N bits at the excluded q (0 at 3/1000, N = 16; 2.5N at 1/5, N = 8 and 24; lane ENGINE band_check.txt, fix-pass f4_x04.py; its forced part is the BEC-X04 rule); 1 <= Z_N <= Z_1(q)^N bounds the numerator excess by ceil(3N log2(b/(b-a))) + 1; Var has about twice the bits. Exact cost: engine A about 1.46e-3 M^5 (log2 b)^2 limb operations for the whole run to M; engine B (D19) needs a Var modulus of 4 beta + 4L bits at N_max, beta = bits(D_N) + ceil(3N log2(b/(b-a))), L = ceil(log2 max(N, 2)), so about (4 beta + 4L)/30.98 primes, each running the recursion to M at 136 to 541 ns per multiply-add (measured at the excluded q = 5/8 for M = 512 down to 64, CPython 3.13.16 on a 4-core Xeon at 2.10 GHz), plus rational reconstruction by the plain extended Euclidean algorithm at about 10.7 s per 413k-bit modulus, quadratic in size, and CRT at about 0.8 s per 413k bits; this gives about 63 min for q = 3/4 to N = 512 (about 102,135 primes; the Var modulus there is 3,164,124 bits and the others about 1,582,000) and about 72 min for the grid on one core (fix-pass f7_mm_smoke.py, f9_mm_cost.py); the earlier engine-B rate of 2.0e-12 to 2.8e-12 s times N^5 (log2 b)^2 was for exact big-rational arithmetic, which engine B no longer does. Interval cost: N(N+1)/2 term updates per pass per recursion; engine B measured 2.1 us per term (both ends, p = 128) at excluded t = 5/7|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D18|text=width under a loose q bracket (lane ENGINE D8): ln Z(q_hi) - ln Z(q_lo) is about <E> ln(q_hi/q_lo), and S behaves the same, so W_q is about 2 f <E> delta for a relative q widening delta; below T_c, <E> is about 3 zeta(4) t^4 N^(4/3)/zeta(3)^(4/3) = 2.5405 t^4 N^(4/3); at excluded (t, N) = (5/7, 3375) this gave crit 0.53 against 0.59 measured; with the production q bracket (delta <= 2^-126) the q part of the width is far below the rounding part|json=0
BECDERIV|id=D19|text=multi-modular exact arithmetic (engine B; fix pass). For a prime p that divides neither b nor any c_k, k <= M, the map n/d -> n d^(p-2) mod p is a ring map on the rationals whose denominators are prime to p; construction 2 uses only +, x and division by c_k^3 and by m <= M, so run mod p it gives E_m mod p, Z_N mod p and every quantity built from them. <N_0>, <N_0^2>, Var(N_0) and the P_j also divide by Z_N = X_N/D_N, and D_N is a unit mod p, so a prime with X_N = 0 mod p (Z_N not invertible mod p) is not used for the quantities at that N that divide by Z_N: it is listed per N in the head row and the next prime is used instead, and p* moves to the next prime below it in the same case. Integers: Y_m = E_m D_m and X_N = Z_N D_N are positive integers below 2^beta, beta = bits(D_N) + ceil(3N log2(b/(b-a))) (D16, D17), so CRT over primes whose product exceeds 2^(beta+1) returns them; CRT uses only per-prime inverses pow(y, p - 2, p) and a product tree. Fractions: a reduced n/d with abs(n) <= A and 0 < d <= B is the only fraction with numerator and denominator below sqrt(Mm/2) congruent to the CRT value u mod Mm when Mm > 2 max(A, B)^2 (Wang); the extended Euclidean algorithm on (Mm, u), stopped at the first remainder below sqrt(Mm/2), returns it, and a returned cofactor d prime to every prime of Mm makes n/d reduced. Bounds, with L = ceil(log2 max(N, 2)) (ceil(log2 N) alone would leave the Var bound one bit short at N = 1, where B <= X_1^2 dominates A): the product of the primes must be at least 2^(2 beta + 1) for Z_N (A = X_N, B = D_N), 2^(2 beta + 2L + 1) for <N_0> (A <= N X_N, B <= X_N), 2^(2 beta + 4L + 1) for <N_0^2> (A <= N^2 X_N, B <= X_N) and 2^(4 beta + 4L) for Var(N_0) (A <= (N^2/4) X_N^2 by Popoviciu, B <= X_N^2); each gives Mm > 2 max(A, B)^2. Distribution: n'_j = Y_(N-j) D_N/D_(N-j) over X_N; with Z_N = r/t reduced, gcd(X_N, D_N) = D_N/t, and since n'_N = D_N the joint gcd g divides D_N/t, a number of at most bits(D_N) - bits(t) + 1 bits (the gcd loss of BEC-X04); g comes from engine B's own Euclid on the n'_j reduced mod D_N/t; then d = X_N/g and n_j = n'_j/g, exact divisions that are checked. Checks: every value against one extra prime p* (the largest prime below 2^30 that divides no c_k and not b), so a wrong reconstruction passes only with probability about 2^-30; and its defining identity cross-multiplied in integers: num(Z) D_N = den(Z) X_N; num(N0) X_N = den(N0) sum_j j n'_j; num(N0SQ) X_N = den(N0SQ) sum_j j^2 n'_j; num(Var) X_N^2 = den(Var) (X_N sum_j j^2 n'_j - (sum_j j n'_j)^2); sum_j n_j = d. The integer identities catch a fault in the CRT or Euclid code; a wrong residue need not fail them, so the extra prime is the check that catches it (in verifier round 1's keepprime smoke at the excluded 2/7 both checks failed at N = 40 and 42, v5_multimod.py). Smoke at the excluded q = 5/8 (M = 64) and 2/7 (M = 48) only: all 40 fractions equal to the exact Fraction values, 0 extra-prime failures; with half the primes all 20 fractions at each q failed the extra-prime check; with a kept prime that divides c_28 (5/8) or c_40 (2/7), exactly the fractions at N >= 28 (N = 32, 64) or N >= 40 (N = 48) failed it and none passed wrongly; a composite modulus can return the true fraction times a factor of the composite and then pass, so a composite is not used as a plant (fix-pass f7_mm_smoke.py, f8_badprime_search.py)|json=0
BECCITE|id=R1|tag=REPORTED|source=H. D. Politzer, Phys. Rev. A 54, 5048 (1996), condensate fluctuations of a trapped ideal Bose gas|verified=0|note=cited as the owner gave it; text not read; only the leading canonical law Var(N_0) ~ (zeta(2)/zeta(3)) t^3 N is attributed|used_by=BEC-T08 (leading law), BEC-L05 (context)|json=0
